import json,statistics,sys
from pathlib import Path
H=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;sys.path.insert(0,str(H.parent/'m4_remeasure'));from campaign import notice
s=json.loads((H/'summary.json').read_text())
caption='Machine/conditions: Apple M4 Mac mini, 10 GPU cores, 24 GiB unified, macOS 15.4.1, AC power, VM untouched. Decimal GB/s; same real Qwen3 28,584×32,768 B streams. GPU lock and idle checks; foreign GPU activity usually exceeds 2%, required retries retained and the second attempt selected. Raw conditions/frequency/accounting are in each log.'
body='Source and evidence ready for audit: final strategy B1; no docs or cost-model values changed.\n\n'
body+='## Files and implementation\n\n'
for p in s['files_written_by_this_task']:body+='- `'+p+'`\n'
body+='\nNew evidence/drivers are under `scratchpad/gpu/metal/m4_ship/`; aggregate `summary.json` lists all files and cache cleanup paths. The separately observed `lmz/gpu/vulkan.py`, `lmz/gpu/lmz_rans.comp` and new `tests/test_vulkan.py` belong to concurrent Vulkan work; I did not edit those files. CUDA .cu source remains identical to the index. Read-only git show was used only for the explicitly requested HEAD baseline snapshot; no git state-changing commands.\n\n'
body+='E1: old kernel retained; new per-stream stride body is HEAD verbatim, with loop bound rounded to four streams so SIMD ballots/barriers stay in lockstep. IOKit core discovery, fallback candidate scale 8; env override LMZ_METAL_PERSTREAM_TGS. Once per process on the first substantial actual caller batch: five candidates (4/8/10/16×device cores and full grid), one warmup plus median of three GPU times. Every candidate’s full output is checked against old-kernel output in chunks, oracle allocation ≤256 MiB. The initial small-slice tuner chose a poor 1024-TG setting; rejected and replaced by this full-traffic method. Asynchronous raw-device calls consume the cached geometry and do not trigger tuning.\n\n'
body+='E3: sized host/device ABIs, correct coded extent from offsets/lengths, retained Python buffer exports (including read-only mappings), caller-owned page buffers or owned Metal-backed memoryviews, and reusable prepare_metal_batch(...).decode() bindings. Prepared views retain bindings and share output: the next decode overwrites prior views. Preparation performs one decode and may tune; cold cost is separate.\n\n'
body+='B1 slack policy: logical coded extent is max(offset+length), distinct from a larger exported capacity. An aligned coded base shares its mapped last page only if page_round_up(extent)−extent ≥ lmz_gpu_pad_bytes() (576); the Metal buffer spans that rounded page range. Otherwise a zero-padded allocation is copied. Inputs/mapped tails must remain valid and unchanged. Host offset ranges, minimum lengths, header magic/frequency sum, output capacities/device maximum remain checked. Raw device callers own padding/lifetime under the CUDA-style trust model. Corrupt payloads can fail a command buffer/return None; caller-owned output may be partially modified, so success must be checked.\n\n'
body+='ARC is now required/enabled (plus IOKit framework linkage). Without ARC, bridge-retained prepared dictionaries were freed at pool exit (observed crash), while new-buffer objects leaked. ARC and retained-view lifetime tests fix that. make_mtl_buffer retains the Python export until release; exact-page alias tests compare the actual Metal contents pointer. The old raw-device ABI declared only 128*nstr+pad coded bytes; corrected. I did not deliberately run its unsafe real 32-KiB case; compared old/new on a fitting 128-byte-plane shared workload instead.\n\n'
body+='E2: callable C/Python decode_bf16_fused and prepared fused bindings. Actual CODEC_BF16 archives have ordinary per-chunk exponent headers; the opt-in chunk path adopts that 516-byte header as its single-stream shared table. It does not invent an archive-shared BF16 format or reinterpret CODEC_SPLIT_ST byte planes. LMZ_METAL_FUSED_CHUNK=1 only; default OFF because every measured per-chunk case is much slower than native CPU. Both regular and fused paths return buffer-compatible memoryviews.\n\n'
body+='## Kernel choice and cost validity\n\nDivergent guarded loads cost about 10%; branchless clamp B2 still cost 4–7% in the same-session matrix and was rejected. Final Metal file begins with the complete HEAD source byte-for-byte, then the padded-bound E1 stride kernel. Existing k values remain valid; I did not rewrite cost_model or docs, per your final instruction.\n\n'+caption+'\n\n| Kernel / G | HEAD kernel GB/s | Final B1 kernel GB/s | Ratio |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n'
for x in s['kernel_b1_comparison']:body+=f"| {x['mode']} / {x['G']} | {x['HEAD_gbps']:.5f} | {x['B1_gbps']:.5f} | {x['ratio']:.5f} |\n"
body+='\nAll four checks differ by <0.8%; raw rejected and accepted matrices: kernel-runs.jsonl, kernel-b2-runs.jsonl, kernel-b1-runs.jsonl. The default per-chunk geometry changes; the old full-grid compatibility row above is not the new selected grid.\n\n'
body+='## API rates, with CPU-read policy explicit\n\n'+caption+' API wall time. Seven timed samples after warmups. HEAD uses unmodified git-show library built with its original non-ARC flags; each timed HEAD sample follows two warmups in a short-lived process to avoid accumulated retained-buffer leaks. New prepared rows use two warmups then seven calls in one process. HEAD wait timing is instrumented externally, without editing HEAD library/source. Fresh controlled HEAD differs from the historical long-lived 1.98 GB/s result; do not call their memory/ownership conditions identical.\n\n| Arm | API GB/s | Kernel GB/s inside that API | Selected TGs |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n'
for x in s['api_b1']:
 if x['mode'].startswith('head') or x['mode']=='new-aligned':body+=f"| {x['mode']} | {x['api_gbps']['median']:.5f} | {x['kernel_gbps']['median']:.5f} | {x['tuned_tgs']} |\n"
for x in s['api_b1_prepared_resident']+s['api_b1_extra']:body+=f"| {x['mode']} | {x['api_gbps']['median']:.5f} | {x['kernel_gbps']['median']:.5f} | {x['tuned_tgs']} |\n"
body+='\nResident/no-between-read rows check the first and last timed output byte-identical; intermediate outputs stay on the GPU. This matches the intended GPU-consumer use case. Checking/CPU-reading the full output after EVERY call is a different condition: prepared shared 22.59 GB/s and fused 18.19 GB/s in api-b1-runs.jsonl, versus ~24.96/~38.91 when intermediate CPU reads are removed in api-b1-resident-runs.jsonl. Both conditions are preserved, not silently substituted. API and GPU timings show the remaining delay is outside measured kernel time; no unsupported claim about internal driver cause. One-shot bindings/output allocation remain slower than prepared reuse.\n\n'
for x in s['api_b1_prepared_resident']:
 if 'perstream' in x['mode']:body+=f"Prepared per-chunk selected {x['tuned_tgs']} TGs: {x['api_gbps']['median']:.5f} API / {x['kernel_gbps']['median']:.5f} kernel GB/s. Its one-time preparation/tuning took {x['prepare_s']:.3f} s in this process. First-call cost can outweigh warm improvements for a single call; these are not end-to-end cold model-load numbers.\n\n"
body+='Component profile (Swift reproduction of the pre-E3 host-copy sequence; seconds, median): `'+json.dumps(s['old_host_profile_stage_s'])+'`. It includes input padding copy, Metal input copy, output allocation, dispatch/wait, kernel timestamps and final output memcpy. Original make_mtl_buffer already aliased complete page ranges; the batched host decoder did not use it. Existing export/device probe results are in existing-exports.json.\n\n'
body+='## Real CODEC_BF16 single-chunk wall comparison\n\n'+caption+' Real BF16 chunks, byte-identical, seven timed API runs after two warmups; same session. CPU reference uses native decode/plane merge, previous GPU path uses rANS plane plus CPU merge.\n\n| BF16 bytes | CPU native GB/s | GPU two-plane GB/s | Opt-in fused GB/s |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n'
for size in sorted(set(a['bf16_bytes'] for a in s['chunk_api'])):
 vals={a['mode']:a['gbps']['median'] for a in s['chunk_api'] if a['bf16_bytes']==size};body+=f"| {size} | {vals['cpu-neon']:.6f} | {vals['gpu-two-plane']:.6f} | {vals['gpu-fused-optin']:.6f} |\n"
body+='\nFusion is useful for many streams on one table, not today’s one-table-per-chunk archive dispatch. Full real original-BF16 data also pass through new fused APIs, not only the synthetic/real small-chunk comparisons.\n\n'
body+='## Tests and remaining limits\n\n'
for t in s['tests']:body+=f"- {t['mode']}: exit {t['exit_code']}; `{t['tail'][-1]}`.\n"
body+='\nThe final pytest run has no remaining assertion failures. All seven failures are custom Skip exceptions: CUDA-only device test, five missing-fusermount3 cases, and one transient WebDAV mount error 19. This is not a fully green pytest exit; baseline had six skip-as-fail and the extra WebDAV skip is retained honestly. New Metal tests pass, and concurrent Vulkan tests pass 105 subtests. No-GPU standalone exits zero and includes the mapped-export Skip fix and all three new tests.\n\n'
body+='Tests cover 1/2/3/5/17/129/1023/1025/4093/4097 streams at full and forced 1/3/7 grids; final real 28,584-stream correctness at the same forced grids; mapped page with ≥576 tail bytes before PROT_NONE uses no-copy, exact page-boundary stream uses padded copy, physical/declared truncation does not crash/hang and a subsequent valid decode works; retained prepared-view lifetime; fused and opt-in/off chunk identities. The repeat pytest wrapper adapts ONLY the new Vulkan fixture’s parent-lock naming (no test filters); standard first run exposed a lock-on-own-parent deadlock. The only legacy Vulkan-test edit here is recognizing masked bitCount as an alternative to the obsolete required intrinsic name.\n\n'
body+='Could not verify an uncontended rate because desktop GPU clients persisted; every over-2% row is retried once and kept flagged. No unsafe real-workload run of the HEAD raw-device extent bug. No cold end-to-end load/inference claim. No guarantee that one process-cached grid is optimal for later workload shapes or changed desktop load; override is available. Docs/limitations/cost_model/README are left to the manager as requested. Cache remains in place; summary.json lists cleanup files.\n'
notice('E final checkpoint',body)
